2. Decision Drivers
Strict Data Isolation: The absolute necessity to guarantee tenant context (e.g., Direct Bank vs. General Bank) is resolved accurately and securely before any interaction with downstream SQL Server databases.

Developer Velocity: We need to eliminate boilerplate "pass-through" code for simple operations while providing a dedicated space for complex UI orchestration.

Security Centralization: A requirement to validate JWTs and authorize requests at the edge of the network rather than inside every individual microservice.

Complex Workflow Orchestration: The introduction of the Flowable workflow engine requires a layer capable of coordinating multi-domain state changes without leaking that complexity to the frontend.

3. Considered Options
Option 1: Strict BFF (Backend-for-Frontend Only)

Description: Every single UI request must route through a BFF layer, with no exceptions.

Outcome: Rejected. While this provides ultimate decoupling, it requires UI and Backend teams to write and maintain boilerplate orchestration code even for simple 1:1 CRUD operations, severely reducing time-to-market.

Option 2: Direct-to-Domain (No Orchestration Layer)

Description: UI applications call core domain services directly for all operations.

Outcome: Rejected. This forces the UI to manage complex orchestration (especially around Flowable processes), leads to massive over-fetching of data, and tightly couples the frontend to backend data models.

Option 3: Smart API Gateway (Logic at the Edge)

Description: Push payload shaping, data aggregation, and orchestration logic directly into the Ingress Controller / API Gateway using scripting or plugins.

Outcome: Rejected. This makes the Gateway a monolithic bottleneck, utilizes proprietary scripting languages, and degrades the developer experience compared to building maintainable orchestration in Spring Boot.

Option 4: Layered API Strategy with Edge Routing (Chosen)

Description: Use the Edge/Ingress for security and routing, a BFF for complex UI orchestration, and allow direct-to-domain calls strictly for simple CRUD operations.
